                "latex": "21",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p25-q6-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Every common divisor divides 21; the ninth column contains 21 and 42, whose GCD is 21.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $k=0,1,2,\\ldots$. The paired terms are $a=5+2k$ and $b=2+5k$. A common divisor of $a$ and $b$ divides every integer multiple and difference of them. Now\n$$\\begin{align*}5a-2b&=5(5+2k)-2(2+5k)\\\\5a-2b&=21.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)$ divides 21, so it cannot exceed 21.\n\nFor $k=8$, the ninth column has\n$$\\begin{align*}a&=5+2(8)\\\\a&=21,\\\\b&=2+5(8)\\\\b&=42.\\end{align*}$$\nSubtracting 21 from 42 gives the equal pair $(21,21)$, so $\\operatorname{GCD}(21,42)=21$. Therefore, the largest number in the third row is $21$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
